Title: Raid log carries a raid id so concurrent raids do not mix in one sitrep
Created: 2026-10-28
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-10-28T14:08Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context
The raid log has one path per repository
(`$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/idh-raid/raid.log`, ticket 1246), and its
lines carry no raid id. On 2026-10-28 two raids ran at once: session
`a369ec1d` (tickets 1299–1301, `RAID_START` 09:55) and session `ebfa5b29`
(`RAID_START` 11:27, while PR 1858 was still at gate round 1). Each raid's
`raid-sitrep.py` reads every line since the last `RAID_START`, so it reports
the other raid's PRs, queue and breakers, and the second `RAID_START` cut off
the first raid's view. The same pattern shows at 09:39/09:55. Found while
checking ticket 1246's cadence (cadence itself held: about 15 min, one
31-minute gap in 30 intervals).

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Every raid-log line carries a raid id set at `RAID_START` (e.g. the
      session id, or a short random id the coordinator exports)
- [ ] `raid-sitrep.py` filters on its raid id; a line without one counts as
      UNREADABLE for the new format, not as data
- [ ] Test: two interleaved raids in one fixture log give two separate sitreps
- [ ] `references/sitrep.md` documents the field
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Raid annotation (Imagine+Plan+Feasibility, 2026-10-28, sonnet seat)

**Premise: holds, worse than stated.** `scripts/raid_log.py:18,33-41` has no
raid field; `scripts/raid-sitrep.py` takes `starts[-1]` for time (70-74) and
reads ALL events for PRs, waves, SITREP marks, breakers and queue (85-142), so a
sitrep lists every earlier raid's PRs (reproduced 2026-10-28 in raid 1310's
own first sitrep). No merged fix.

**Path.** `raid=<id>` is one more key=value. `append RAID_START` mints
`secrets.token_hex(3)` unless `raid=` is given, and prints the line (the
coordinator reads the id there). Any other event without `raid` raises → exit 2.
`raid-sitrep.py --raid <id>` (required; missing → UNREADABLE raid, exit 2)
filters events before every derived list; no RAID_START for the id → existing
UNREADABLE RAID_START path, also when the log holds only other raids; the SITREP
mark carries the raid. Legacy lines without `raid=` are ignored, never
UNREADABLE. Refuse: env-only id (Bash exports do not persist; the background
`sleep 900; sitrep` needs the id baked in), per-raid files, registry/locks,
backfill, "latest raid" guessing, a Raid class.

**Actions.** (a) first failing test
`test_two_interleaved_raids_give_separate_sitreps`; (b) raid_log.py; (c)
raid-sitrep.py; (d) fixture and existing tests carry `raid=`; (e)
references/sitrep.md (field row, Reading note) and SKILL.md line 54 and
§ Sitrep cadence ("read the id RAID_START prints; `raid=<id>` on every append;
arm `sleep 900; raid-sitrep.py --raid <id>`"); keep the phrase "write
`RAID_START` and arm the sitrep cadence" (tests/test_raid_sitrep.py:203). Check
how the `<!-- inline: references/sitrep.md -->` copy is kept in sync. (f) `make check`.

**Tests that catch a wrong implementation.** One fixture, two interleaved raids
with distinct data (A: PR 10 at gate_round_1, a breaker, queue 2; B: PR 20
merged, a SITREP mark, queue 0, a later RAID_START). Assert each output has its
own PR and NEXT line and not the other's; B's later RAID_START does not cut A's
time line; B's SITREP mark does not hide A's breakers; legacy raid-less lines
appear in neither and cause no UNREADABLE; unknown/missing `--raid` exit 2;
append without raid raises; two mints differ and match LINE.

**Feasibility: PASS.** No open PR touches these files. WARN: the raid running
this ticket logged its RAID_START without an id; its sitrep becomes UNREADABLE
once this lands (the coordinator re-logs with an id). Related: tracker 1309
(runtime.py) will later carry the wake for this cadence.
